{
  "image_name": "1ea68b6a87228c5ba1f43acdba1b7e34.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several long-read RNA sequencing library preparation strategies, highlighting how full-length mRNA is converted or adapted for nanopore or PacBio sequencing. A central theme is preserving transcript ends and transcript-specific features while adding platform-specific adapters.\n\nSeveral methods begin with reverse transcription of poly(A)+ mRNA into cDNA. One schematic shows standard full-length cDNA generation by reverse transcription, template switching, and PCR amplification. Other workflows depict commercial ONT and PacBio approaches, where cDNA molecules receive adapters, motor proteins, barcodes, or circularized structures for sequencing. The PacBio-related diagrams emphasize adapter ligation and, in one method, concatenated or linked cDNA segments with labeled barcode regions.\n\nOther panels focus on enrichment or capture of target transcripts. TEQUILA-seq appears to convert both target and non-target mRNAs to cDNA, then selectively hybridize target molecules to probes, capture them on streptavidin beads, and amplify them. CapTrap and another method using rolling circle amplification or oligonucleotide annealing seem designed to enrich intact full-length molecules or preserve transcript ends.\n\nThe final workflow illustrates direct RNA sequencing by ONT, where native full-length mRNA is ligated to sequencing adapters, optionally reverse transcribed, and sequenced without full cDNA conversion. Overall, the figure compares how different protocols capture full-length transcript information for long-read analysis.",
